  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h1 style="font-size: 36px;">Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules</h1>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <p>For use in preparing <span data-field="year">{{ year }}</span> Returns</p>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 50px;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Important Change for 1999</h2>
      <p>Photographs of missing children. The Internal Revenue Service is a proud partner with the National Center for Missing and Exploited Children. Photographs of missing children selected by the Center may appear in this publication on pages that would otherwise be blank. You can help bring these children home by looking at the photographs and calling 1-800-THE-LOST (1-800-843-5678) if you recognize a child.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Introduction</h2>
      <p>This publication discusses two sets of rules that may limit the losses you can deduct on your tax return from any trade or business, rental, or other income-producing activity. The first part of the publication contains the passive activity rules. The second part discusses the at-risk rules. However, when you figure out your allowable losses from any activity, you must apply the at-risk rules before the passive activity rules.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-top: 20px;">Useful Items</h3>
      <p>You may want to see:</p>
      <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><span data-field="link_to_publication_1">{{ link_to_publication_1 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="link_to_publication_2">{{ link_to_publication_2 }}</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Passive Activity Limits</h2>
    <p>Generally, you are in a passive activity if you have a trade or business activity in which you materially participate, or if you have a rental activity. These terms are defined in this publication.</p>
    <p>If you have a loss, you must determine the <span data-field="term_at_risk">{{ term_at_risk }}</span> in the activity. The at-risk amount is explained in the next part of this publication. After you figure out your at-risk amount, you must apply the passive activity loss rules to see if you can deduct the loss.</p>
    <p>In general, you can deduct passive activity losses only from the tax year in which they occur. If you have a loss in a passive activity, you can carry that loss forward to the next tax year. Also, you can carry forward losses to the next tax year if you are not materially participating in the activity. You can also deduct losses from the activity in a fully taxable transaction. See Dispositions.</p>
    <p>Passive activity credits. You can subtract passive activity credits only from the tax year in which they occur. If you have a credit in a passive activity, you can carry that credit forward to the next tax year. Also, you can carry forward credits to the next tax year if you are not materially participating in the activity. See Credits.</p>
    <p>Unallocated passive activity credits. Unlike unallocated passive activity losses, are not carried forward to the next tax year. You can only use these credits in the tax year in which they occur. See Credits.</p>
    <p>See the instructions for Form 8582-CT for more information on how to apply the passive activity loss rules to PTPs, and on how to apply the limit on passive activity losses.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Activities That Are Not Passive</h2>
    <p>The following are not passive activities:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Trade or business activities in which you materially participate.</li>
      <li>Rental activities in which you materially participate.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Rental Activities</h2>
    <p>A rental activity is a passive activity even if you materially participate in it, unless you materially participate as a real estate professional. A real estate professional is an individual who materially participates in real property trades or businesses in which they qualify as a real estate professional. See Real Estate Professional.</p>
    <p>Exceptions. Your activity is not a rental activity if:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The average period of customer use of your property is less than 15 days.</li>
      <li>The average period of customer use of your property is more than 15 days, and you provide services to customers in the course of your activity.</li>
      <li>The average period of customer use of your property is more than 15 days, and you provide services to customers in the course of your activity, and you materially participate in the activity.</li>
      <li>The rental is incidental to a nonrental activity.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>For more information on how to apply the passive activity loss rules to PTPs, and on how to apply the limit on passive activity losses, see Form 8582-CT.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Real Estate Professional</h2>
    <p>Generally, rental activities are passive activities. However, if you qualify as a real estate professional, rental real estate activities are not passive activities. To qualify as a real estate professional, you must meet the following requirements:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>You must materially participate in real property trades or businesses for more than 750 hours during the tax year.</li>
      <li>You must perform more than 50% of the total personal services performed in all of your trades or businesses during the tax year.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If you qualify as a real estate professional, you can deduct losses from rental real estate activities, provided that the losses are not suspended under other rules.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Passive Activity Deductions</h2>
    <p>Passive activity deductions include all deductions that are allowable for a passive activity. However, you can only deduct passive activity losses to the extent of your passive activity income. Passive activity deductions do not include:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The trade or business activity is unsubstantial in relation to the rental activity.</li>
      <li>Each owner of the trade or business materially participates in the rental activity in which the case pertains.</li>
      <li>The rental activity involves the use of the trade or business activity may be grouped with the trade or business activity as described in this publication.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Example, Herbert and Wilma are Married Filing Jointly</h2>
    <p>Herbert and Wilma are married filing jointly. Herbert owns a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property.</p>
    <p>Because Herbert and Wilma file a joint return, they are treated as one taxpayer for passive activity purposes. Herbert's rental property is a passive activity. Wilma's rental property is a passive activity. Herbert's property is a rental property. Wilma's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Wilma's property is a rental property.</p>
    <p>Herbert's property is a rental property. Wilma's property is a rental property. Herbert's property is a rental property. Wilma's property is a rental property.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Worksheet A. Significant Participation Passive Activities</h2>
    <p><table>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 30%;">Name of Activity</th>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Hours of Participation</th>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Net Loss</th>
        <th style="width: 30%;">Net Income</th>
      </tr>
    </table></p>
    <p style="margin-top: 20px;"><table>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Column (a). Enter net income, if any, from the activity. Net income means the excess of gross income over deductions.</th>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Column (b). Enter net loss, if any, from the activity. Net loss means the excess of deductions over gross income.</th>
      </tr>
    </table></p>
    <p style="margin-top: 20px;"><table>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Column (c). Enter the total of columns (a) and (b).</th>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Column (d). Combine totals of cols. (b) and (c).</th>
      </tr>
    </table></p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Rental of Nondepreciable Property</h2>
    <p>If you rent nondepreciable property, you may have passive activity income or loss. If you have passive activity income, you can deduct it against other income. If you have passive activity loss, you may be able to deduct it against other income if you qualify as a real estate professional.</p>
    <p>For more information, see Regulations Section 1.469-2(f).</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Rental of Property to a Nonpassive Activity</h2>
    <p>If you rent property to a trade or business activity, the rental activity may be passive or not passive. If the rental activity is passive, you may be able to deduct losses only to the extent of your passive activity income. If the rental activity is not passive, you may be able to deduct losses against other income.</p>
    <p>For more information, see Regulations Section 1.469-2(f).</p>
  </div>

</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Worksheet B. List only the significant passive activities that have net losses.</h2>
      <p>Column (a). Enter the name of the activity. Column (b). Enter the amount of the net loss. Column (c). Enter the amount of the net income. Column (d). Enter the total of columns (b) and (c). Column (e). Enter the amount of the net loss that is recharacterized as passive income.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Grouping Your Activities</h2>
      <p>Grouping is a method of combining two or more trades or businesses into a single activity. You may group activities if they meet certain requirements. If you group activities, you must treat them as a single activity for passive activity purposes.</p>
      <p>You may group activities if:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>The activities are related to each other.</li>
        <li>The activities are conducted by the same person.</li>
        <li>The activities are conducted in the same location.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If you group activities, you must treat them as a single activity for passive activity purposes. You must also report the combined activity on your tax return.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Appropriate Economic Units</h2>
    <p>Generally, to consent that an activity is a single economic unit, you must meet certain requirements. These requirements include:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The activities are related to each other.</li>
      <li>The activities are conducted by the same person.</li>
      <li>The activities are conducted in the same location.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If you meet these requirements, you can treat the activities as a single economic unit for passive activity purposes.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Activities Conducted Through Another Entity</h2>
    <p>If you conduct an activity through another entity, you may be treated as if you conducted the activity yourself. This is called "disregarded entity" treatment. You must determine if the entity is disregarded for tax purposes. If it is, you must include the activity in your personal return.</p>
    <p>With each other:</p>
    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
      <li>With activities conducted directly by you.</li>
      <li>With activities conducted through other entities.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>You may not treat activities conducted through another entity as if you conducted them yourself if:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The entity is a partnership.</li>
      <li>The entity is a corporation.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Personal Service and Closely Held Corporations</h2>
    <p>You may group an activity with a personal service activity or a closely held corporation activity if you meet certain requirements. These requirements include:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The activities are related to each other.</li>
      <li>The activities are conducted by the same person.</li>
      <li>The activities are conducted in the same location.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If you meet these requirements, you can treat the activities as a single economic unit for passive activity purposes.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Partial Dispositions</h2>
    <p>If you dispose of a portion of an activity, you may be treated as if you disposed of the entire activity. This is called "partial disposition" treatment. You must determine if the partial disposition is treated as a full disposition for tax purposes. If it is, you must report the disposition on your tax return.</p>
    <p>For more information, see Regulations Section 1.469-2(f).</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Recharacterization</h2>
    <p>Not all income or loss from a passive activity is treated as passive. Some income or loss may be recharacterized. Recharacterization means that the income or loss is treated as if it came from a different type of activity. This can happen if you meet certain requirements.</p>
    <p>Significant participation passive activities. These are activities in which you materially participate. If you materially participate in an activity, the income or loss from that activity is not passive.</p>
    <p>For more information, see Regulations Section 1.469-2(f).</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Worksheet A. Significant Participation Passive Activities</h2>
    <p><table>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 30%;">Name of Activity</th>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Hours of Participation</th>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Net Loss</th>
        <th style="width: 30%;">Net Income</th>
      </tr>
    </table></p>
    <p style="margin-top: 20px;"><table>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Column (a). Enter net income, if any, from the activity. Net income means the excess of gross income over deductions.</th>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Column (b). Enter net loss, if any, from the activity. Net loss means the excess of deductions over gross income.</th>
      </tr>
    </table></p>
    <p style="margin-top: 20px;"><table>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Column (c). Enter the total of columns (a) and (b).</th>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Column (d). Combine totals of cols. (b) and (c).</th>
      </tr>
    </table></p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Worksheet B. List only the significant passive activities that have net losses.</h2>
    <p><table>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 30%;">Name of Activity</th>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Amount of Net Loss</th>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Amount of Net Income</th>
        <th style="width: 30%;">Total of Columns (b) and (c)</th>
      </tr>
    </table></p>
    <p style="margin-top: 20px;"><table>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Column (e). Enter the amount of the net loss that is recharacterized as passive income.</th>
      </tr>
    </table></p>
  </div>

</div>



