<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">At-Risk Limits</h2>
      <p>The at-risk rules limit your losses from most activities to your amount at risk in the activity. You treat any loss that is disallowed by the at-risk rules as a prior year unallowed loss, which you carry forward to the next tax year. If your amount at risk is reduced below zero, you must report the excess loss as a prior year unallowed loss.</p>
      <p>You must apply the at-risk rules before the passive activity rules, as discussed in the first part of this publication.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="text-align: right;">
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Page <span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Loss defined.</strong> A loss is the excess of all allowable deductions from the activity for the year over all allowable income from the activity for the year. Income does not include income from the receipt of capital gains, dividends, interest, or return of capital.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>At-Risk Rules.</strong></p>
    <p>You must apply the at-risk rules before the passive activity rules, as discussed in the first part of this publication.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>1. Stock owned directly or indirectly by or for a corporation, partnership, estate, or trust.</strong></p>
    <p>If you own stock directly or indirectly by or for a corporation, partnership, estate, or trust, you are at risk to the extent of your investment in that stock.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>2. An individual is considered to own the stock directly or indirectly by or for a corporation, partnership, estate, or trust if:</strong></p>
    <ol>
      <li>The individual is a shareholder, partner, estate, or trust.</li>
      <li>The individual is a beneficiary of a trust.</li>
      <li>The individual is a spouse of a shareholder, partner, estate, or trust.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>3. If a person holds an option to buy stock, they are at risk to the extent of the option's exercise price.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>4. When applying rule (1), (2), or (3) as treated above, the amount at risk is the amount of the individual's investment in the stock or the option.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>5. Stock that may be considered owned by an individual under rule (2) or (3) is not at risk to the extent of the amount of the individual's investment in the stock or the option.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>6. The amount at risk is the amount of the individual's investment in the stock or the option.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Exceptions for holding real property placed in service before 1987.</strong></p>
    <p>The at-risk rules do not apply to real property placed in service before 1987. The at-risk rules apply to real property placed in service after 1987.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Exceptions for leasing by a corporation.</strong></p>
    <p>A corporation is not at risk for losses from a lease of real property if the corporation is engaged in a trade or business other than real property leasing. If the corporation is engaged in real property leasing, the at-risk rules apply.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Activities Covered.</strong></p>
    <p>If you are involved in one or more of the following activities, the at-risk rules apply to your losses:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Farming.</li>
      <li>Exploring for, or exploiting, oil and gas.</li>
      <li>Mining, quarrying, or drilling for minerals.</li>
      <li>Leasing real property.</li>
      <li>Operating a business that is not a trade or business of the corporation.</li>
      <li>Any other activity not included in (1) through (5) that is carried on as a trade or business for the production of income.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Section 1245 property.</strong></p>
    <p>Section 1245 property includes property that is held for the production of income, such as equipment and furniture.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Special exception for qualified corp-oration.</strong></p>
    <p>A qualified corporation is a closely held corporation. A closely held corporation is one that is owned by five or fewer individuals, or by individuals who own 50% or more of the corporation.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Activities Covered.</strong></p>
    <p>If you are involved in one or more of the following activities, the at-risk rules apply to your losses:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Farming.</li>
      <li>Exploring for, or exploiting, oil and gas.</li>
      <li>Mining, quarrying, or drilling for minerals.</li>
      <li>Leasing real property.</li>
      <li>Operating a business that is not a trade or business of the corporation.</li>
      <li>Any other activity not included in (1) through (5) that is carried on as a trade or business for the production of income.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Section 1245 property.</strong></p>
    <p>Section 1245 property includes property that is held for the production of income, such as equipment and furniture.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Special exception for qualified corp-oration.</strong></p>
    <p>A qualified corporation is a closely held corporation. A closely held corporation is one that is owned by five or fewer individuals, or by individuals who own 50% or more of the corporation.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Who Is Affected?</h2>
      <p>The at-risk limits apply to individuals (includ-ing partners and S corporation shareholders) and to certain closely held corporations (other than S corporations).</p>
    </div>
    <div style="text-align: right;">
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Page <span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Closely held corporation.</strong></p>
    <p>For the at-risk rules, a corporation is a closely held corp-oration if it is owned by five or fewer individuals, or by individuals who own 50% or more of the stock. If the stock is owned by five or fewer individuals, apply the following rules.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>1) During the entire 12-month period end-ing on the last day of the tax year, the corporation had at least:</strong></p>
    <ol>
      <li>One full-time employee whose ser-vice is performed in the United States.</li>
      <li>Three full-time nonemployer employees who do not own more than 5% of the corporation at any time during the year.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>2) The corporation's total assets are not more than $10 million.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>3) The corporation's total assets are not more than $10 million.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Separation of Activities</strong></p>
    <p>Generally, you treat activity income or loss as being from a single activity. If you have two or more activities that are related, you must treat them as a single activity.</p>
    <p>For a partnership or S corporation, treat all income or loss from all activities as being from a single activity.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Aggregation of Activities</strong></p>
    <p>Trade or business activities described in section 8 (under Activities Covered) are aggregated for the at-risk rules.</p>
    <p>1) You actively participate in the man-agement of the trade or business.</p>
    <p>2) The trade or business is carried on by a partnership or S corporation. The partnership or S corporation is actively managed.</p>
    <p>3) Similar rules apply to activities described in section 1245 property.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Active participation.</strong></p>
    <p>Active participation, active participation de-pends on all the facts and circumstances. You must determine whether you actively participate in the man-agement of the activity, performing the services for the activity, and hing-ing.</p>
  </div>
</div>

